    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0001-b0008",
      "page_number": 1,
      "reading_order": 8,
      "block_type": "bullet",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Explain how climate affects human life and the environment.",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 13,
        "source_line_end": 13,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading": "Learning Objectives"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0001-b0009",
      "page_number": 1,
      "reading_order": 9,
      "block_type": "section_heading",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Start-Up",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 15,
        "source_line_end": 15,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading_level": 2,
      "heading": "Start-Up"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0001-b0010",
      "page_number": 1,
      "reading_order": 10,
      "block_type": "paragraph",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Look outside the window! Is it sunny, cloudy, or rainy? Have you ever noticed how the weather changes throughout the day? Some places are always warm, while others are cold. Why does this happen? In this chapter, we will explore the differences between weather and climate and understand the factors that influence them.",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 17,
        "source_line_end": 17,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading": "Start-Up"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0001-b0011",
      "page_number": 1,
      "reading_order": 11,
      "block_type": "section_heading",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Weather",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 19,
        "source_line_end": 19,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading_level": 2,
      "heading": "Weather"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0001-b0012",
      "page_number": 1,
      "reading_order": 12,
      "block_type": "paragraph",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Weather is the condition of the atmosphere at a particular place and time. It includes temperature, wind, rainfall, humidity, and cloud cover. Weather can change from hour to hour or day to day. For example, one morning, it may be sunny and by afternoon it could rain.",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 21,
        "source_line_end": 21,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading": "Weather"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0001-b0013",
      "page_number": 1,
      "reading_order": 13,
      "block_type": "section_heading",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Do You Know?",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 23,
        "source_line_end": 23,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading_level": 2,
      "heading": "Do You Know?"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0001-b0014",
      "page_number": 1,
      "reading_order": 14,
      "block_type": "paragraph",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "The hottest place on Earth is the Lut Desert in Iran, where temperatures have been recorded as high as 159.3°F (70.7°C)! Imagine how hot it must be to have that much heat!",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 25,
        "source_line_end": 25,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading": "Do You Know?"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0002-b0001",
      "page_number": 2,
      "reading_order": 15,
      "block_type": "section_heading",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Climate",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 29,
        "source_line_end": 29,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading_level": 2,
      "heading": "Climate"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0002-b0002",
      "page_number": 2,
      "reading_order": 16,
      "block_type": "paragraph",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Climate is the average weather condition of a place over a long period, usually 30 years or more. It tells us whether a place is generally hot, cold, wet, or dry. For example, the climate of the Sahara Desert is hot and dry, while the climate of the Amazon Rainforest is warm and rainy.",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 31,
        "source_line_end": 31,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading": "Climate"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0002-b0003",
      "page_number": 2,
      "reading_order": 17,
      "block_type": "section_heading",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Factors Affecting Climate",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 33,
        "source_line_end": 33,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading_level": 2,
      "heading": "Factors Affecting Climate"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0002-b0004",
      "page_number": 2,
      "reading_order": 18,
      "block_type": "paragraph",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Several factors influence the climate of a place. Let us explore them one by one.",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 35,
        "source_line_end": 35,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading": "Factors Affecting Climate"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0002-b0005",
      "page_number": 2,
      "reading_order": 19,
      "block_type": "section_heading",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "Distance from the Equator (Latitudes)",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 37,
        "source_line_end": 37,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading_level": 2,
      "heading": "Distance from the Equator (Latitudes)"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0002-b0006",
      "page_number": 2,
      "reading_order": 20,
      "block_type": "paragraph",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
      "source_text": "The Earth is divided into imaginary lines called latitudes. Places near the equator receive direct sunlight throughout the year, making them hot. As we move towards the poles, sunlight spreads out, making these areas cooler. This is why tropical regions are warm, while polar regions are cold.",
      "required_for_coverage": true,
      "provenance": {
        "source_line_start": 39,
        "source_line_end": 39,
        "extraction_mode": "markdown_fallback"
      },
      "heading": "Distance from the Equator (Latitudes)"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "p0002-b0007",
      "page_number": 2,
      "reading_order": 21,
      "block_type": "figure",
      "semantic_role": "lesson_content",
